.TH CCOL_CIRCULAR_QUEUE_DESTROY 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_circular_queue_destroy \- destroy a circular queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_circular_queue_destroy(cq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro frees all the resources of the queue. It then sets the queue
pointer to NULL.

ccol_event_loop_remove() returns without a wait for a callback that it
already gave to a reactor thread. Such a callback can therefore still be
inside this queue when the removal returns. This call blocks until every
one of those callbacks finishes with the queue. Then it frees the queue.
This is what makes the documented teardown sequence safe as written. That
sequence is: drain, remove, destroy.

A callback that the loop runs for a different registration can run this
whole sequence for this queue, with any num_reactor_threads. A dispatch
that the loop collected for it and did not start yet does not hold the
destroy up, and it never runs its callback after the removal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cq   Circular queue to destroy. The macro sets it to NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
You can call this with a NULL pointer.

The macro evaluates cq exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so an expression with a
side effect, such as handles[i++], destroys and clears one element.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Messages that stay in the queue cause an assertion failure.
Drain the queue before you destroy it. The destroy counts the messages
only after every callback that the removal left running finished. A
write callback that was already running when ccol_event_loop_remove()
returned can still send, and that message causes the assertion failure
too. When a write registration watches the queue, remove it, wait for its
on_removed callback, and only then drain and destroy the queue.
A watcher on this queue also causes an assertion failure. A watcher is a
ccol_select() call, a ccol_select_timed() call, or an ccol_event_loop
registration (ccol_selectable_from_circq or ccol_selectable_from_chan).
Without that assertion, the waiter is still linked and points to the mutex
of this queue, which the destroy is about to free.
Call ccol_event_loop_remove() before you destroy the queue. You can also
let every ccol_select() call and every ccol_select_timed() call that
watches the queue return first.
Do not call this from inside an ccol_event_loop callback that dispatches
this queue. This is a caller error, and the program stops through
ccol_fatal_err(). The destroy would wait for that same callback.
Do not hold an application lock across this call if one of these callbacks
takes that lock. The destroy waits for the callback, and the callback
would wait for the lock.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_circular_queue_create (3)
